fca589bbb0 Tue Jul 15 13:56:39 2008 +0000 IntLiteral is now a subtype of ZZ32 (and thus of every other numeric type).  But there are a number of caveats: * ZZ32 is now a trait, with subtypes Int and IntLiteral.  In practice all the ZZ32 math you do will end up yielding results of type Int. This means that when you stuff the results into a polymorphically-typed container (such as Maybe or List) the type variable will invariably be dynamically instantiated with Int, rather than ZZ32 as you'd expect. Most of the changes in this checkin address this in the regression tests. * In order to obtain the expected behavior with large constants, evaluation a too-large IntLiteral actually yields a ZZ.  This means in practice that the interpreter never actually creates an object of type IntLiteral.  The reason this is necessary is because we will otherwise attempt to use ZZ32 arithmetic on any constant that is too big to fit in ZZ64!  You might imagine an alternative workaround where we define redundant math on IntLiterals that yields ZZ, but this will cause all kinds of problems with the overloading rules as it violates subtyping.  In practice programmers shouldn't notice this at all (in stark, stark contrast to the previous bullet). * A couple of test were invalidated.  XXXextendZZ32.fss doesn't spot its failure condition (extending a closed trait); it was previously extending a closed object type and failed as expected.  This has been filed as a bug. SpecData/examples/basic/StatPara.Opr.IDENTITYplus.fss appears to have been fundamentally wrong all along, and we suddenly started spotting and reporting the bug.  It's been deleted as it was unused in the spec.
